winex11: MIT-SHM over the app's fd-passing socket.

bionic exports shmget and friends, so this compiles and links, but Android
refuses them at runtime -- the NDK's own sys/shm.h says "Not useful on Android
because it's disallowed by SELinux". Wine therefore drops to XPutImage, which is
correct but pushes every damaged region through the X socket as a copy.

A SysV shmid could not be used here even if SELinux allowed it. It names a
segment in the kernel IPC namespace, and the X server on the other end is a Java
process inside the app that can only mmap a descriptor. Any MIT-SHM in an
Android app has to be fd-passing.

The app already runs the server half (com.winlator.sysvshm): a unix socket where
SHMGET allocates ashmem and GET_FD returns the descriptor over SCM_RIGHTS, with
the server keeping its own id table so the id in XShmAttach resolves. Three
requests, native byte order, one connection per allocation and per release --
the server does not release anything when a client disconnects, so no connection
has to be held open:

    SHMGET  0  u32 size   -> i32 shmid
    GET_FD  1  i32 shmid  -> u8 status, descriptor via SCM_RIGHTS
    DELETE  2  i32 shmid  -> nothing

Winlator solves the same problem by patching glibc to redirect shmget to this
socket. On bionic there is no libc to patch, so the client half goes here.

Two behavioural differences from the SysV path, both handled:

  - A SysV segment is IPC_RMID'd right after attach so it dies with the last
    detach. The socket server has no such rule, so DELETE is sent from
    destroy_shm_image instead.
  - The mapping length is recovered from the XImage at teardown rather than
    stored, because x11drv_xshm_info_t is Xlib's XShmSegmentInfo and has nowhere
    to keep it.

Gated on WINE_SYSVSHM_SOCKET naming the socket. Unset, every path here is
bypassed and the behaviour is exactly upstream's.

Three one-shot winediag lines report what happened, because the answer is
otherwise unobservable: every failure in here falls back to XPutImage, which is
correct and silent, so a log with no MIT-SHM in it cannot be told from a log
where MIT-SHM was never tried. winediag is on in a default WINEDEBUG, so this
costs nothing to read.

Not suitable upstream as-is: the protocol is specific to this app's helper.

